# Grafana 13.2.1 + Prometheus 3.14.0 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# Two services, two very different configuration models.
#
# GRAFANA (grafana/grafana:13.2.1). The compose used to pin
# grafana/grafana-oss, but that Docker Hub repo stopped publishing after
# 13.0.2 (2026-06-02): 13.0.8 / 13.1.5 / 13.2.1 all 404 there. The maintained
# official repo is grafana/grafana ("The official Grafana docker container",
# rebuilt 2026-09-01), and its Dockerfile still builds with
# GO_BUILD_TAGS="oss" / WIRE_TAGS="oss", so this is the same OSS edition. The
# image name changed, the build did not. The default (alpine) tag is the one
# to use: `apk add ... curl` is only in the `final-alpine` stage, so the
# compose healthcheck (`curl -f .../api/health`) breaks on the -slim and
# -distroless variants.
#
# Grafana has almost no bespoke environment variables. Every key in
# conf/defaults.ini is settable as `GF_<SECTION>_<KEY>`, uppercased with `.`
# and `-` turned into `_` (pkg/setting/setting.go at v13.2.1):
#   EnvKey(section, key) = "GF_" + envNameFromIniName(section) + "_" +
#                          envNameFromIniName(key)
#   envNameFromIniName(n) = ToUpper(n) with "." → "_" and "-" → "_"
# So `[auth.generic_oauth] client_id` is GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID. A
# second pass then scans every remaining `GF_*` variable and CREATES the ini
# key it names, so keys absent from defaults.ini still work. That surface is
# ~2,669 lines of defaults.ini and is not reproduced here: what follows is the
# compose-set keys plus the sections an operator actually touches (server,
# security, users, auth, database, smtp, log, plugins, analytics). Everything
# else is upstream-documented at the configuration reference linked below.
#
# Container-level extras that come from the image's entrypoint
# (packaging/docker/run.sh at v13.2.1), not from defaults.ini: the
# `GF_*__FILE` docker-secret convention, GF_AWS_PROFILES, and the deprecated
# GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS. Note the last one no longer installs anything by
# itself at 13.x: run.sh only acts on it when GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS_FORCE=true,
# and prints a deprecation warning either way. Use GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL.
#
# PROMETHEUS (prom/prometheus:v3.14.0, the newest stable, 2026-08-18; that tag
# and `latest` share digest sha256:5ce7540c…). Be honest: Prometheus is NOT
# configured by environment variables. Scrape targets, rules, remote write and
# alerting live in /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml, and retention/storage/web
# behaviour lives in CLI flags on the container's command. A grep of
# `os.Getenv(` / `os.LookupEnv(` across cmd/ at v3.14.0 returns exactly ONE
# hit: `DEBUG` in cmd/prometheus/main.go. There is no PROMETHEUS_* namespace
# to invent. To change anything real here, edit the compose's `command:` /
# mount a prometheus.yml. The two Prometheus keys below are the whole
# environment surface, plus the Go runtime knobs the binary inherits.
#
# The compose sets GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER and GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD from
# GF_ADMIN_USER / GF_ADMIN_PASSWORD operator inputs, so both halves of each
# pair are declared: the GF_ADMIN_* key is what an operator types, the
# GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_* key is what the container sees.
#
# Source: conf/defaults.ini, pkg/setting/setting.go, Dockerfile and
# packaging/docker/run.sh at grafana v13.2.1; cmd/ and Dockerfile at
# prometheus v3.14.0.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Configure Grafana (grafana.ini reference)", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/)
# ---

# ─── Admin account (set by the compose) ───────────────────────────────────
# @docs("Override configuration with environment variables", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#override-configuration-with-environment-variables)

# Login name of the admin account Grafana creates on first boot, and only on
# first boot. After the database exists, changing this makes a SECOND admin
# rather than renaming the first. Upstream's default is `admin`.
GF_ADMIN_USER=admin

# Password for that admin account, applied at first boot. LEAVE THIS EMPTY AND
# THE INSTANCE IS PUBLIC: Grafana falls back to its documented default of
# `admin`/`admin`, which is the first credential pair every scanner tries, and
# a Grafana admin can add a data source pointing anywhere the container can
# reach. Generate one (`openssl rand -base64 18`) before the first start.
# Rotating it here later does nothing. Change it in the UI, or set
# GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD alongside a reset.
# @required @sensitive
# @docs("Configure security", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#security)
GF_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# What the compose passes to the grafana container as the [security]
# admin_user ini key. Derived from GF_ADMIN_USER above; set that one.
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER=${GF_ADMIN_USER:-admin}

# What the compose passes to the grafana container as the [security]
# admin_password ini key. Derived from GF_ADMIN_PASSWORD above; set that one.
# @required @sensitive
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=${GF_ADMIN_PASSWORD}

# Email recorded for the admin account at creation. Only matters if you want
# password-reset mail to reach a real inbox (needs SMTP below).
# @type=email
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@localhost

# Skip creating the admin account entirely. Only sane when an OAuth/LDAP
# provider is already configured and working. Otherwise nobody can log in.
# @type=boolean
GF_SECURITY_DISABLE_INITIAL_ADMIN_CREATION=false

# ─── Server / URL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("[server] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#server)

# The full public URL browsers use. Grafana builds redirect targets, OAuth
# callbacks, alert notification links and rendered-image URLs from it, so a
# wrong value shows up as "login loops back to localhost". Set it to the
# domain in front of this stack, including the scheme and any subpath.
# @type=url
GF_SERVER_ROOT_URL=

# Public-facing hostname, used to expand the default root_url and for the
# DNS-rebinding check below. Redundant once GF_SERVER_ROOT_URL is set.
GF_SERVER_DOMAIN=localhost

# Rejects requests whose Host header does not match GF_SERVER_DOMAIN
# (DNS-rebinding protection). Turn on only after the domain is correct, or
# every request 404s.
# @type=boolean
GF_SERVER_ENFORCE_DOMAIN=false

# Set to true when GF_SERVER_ROOT_URL contains a path (e.g. /grafana) and the
# proxy does NOT strip that prefix. Getting this backwards is the usual cause
# of a blank page with 404s on /public/build/*.js.
# @type=boolean
GF_SERVER_SERVE_FROM_SUB_PATH=false

# Port Grafana listens on inside the container. The compose exposes container
# port 3000 and the healthcheck curls it, so changing this alone breaks both:
# update the compose's ports and healthcheck too.
# @type=port
GF_SERVER_HTTP_PORT=3000

# Bind address. Empty binds all interfaces, which is what the container
# network needs.
GF_SERVER_HTTP_ADDR=

# http, https, h2 or socket. Leave `http` and terminate TLS at the proxy in
# front; `https` additionally needs GF_SERVER_CERT_FILE / GF_SERVER_CERT_KEY
# mounted into the container.
# @type=enum(http, https, h2, socket, socket_h2)
GF_SERVER_PROTOCOL=http
GF_SERVER_CERT_FILE=
GF_SERVER_CERT_KEY=
# @sensitive
GF_SERVER_CERT_PASS=

# Logs one line per web request. Useful while debugging a proxy, noisy after.
# @type=boolean
GF_SERVER_ROUTER_LOGGING=false

# ─── Security hardening ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("[security] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#security)

# Key used to sign auth cookies and, by default, to encrypt data-source
# credentials in the database. Upstream ships the literal fallback
# `SW2YcwTIb9zpOOhoPsMm`, a published constant, identical on every unset
# install, so anyone can decrypt a stolen database dump. Set a random value
# BEFORE first boot; changing it later invalidates already-encrypted secrets.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Database encryption", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-security/configure-database-encryption/)
GF_SECURITY_SECRET_KEY=

# Marks session cookies Secure. Required once Grafana is served over HTTPS;
# harmless-looking but it silently breaks login over plain HTTP.
# @type=boolean
GF_SECURITY_COOKIE_SECURE=false

# SameSite attribute for the session cookie. `none` (needed for embedding in
# another site's iframe) also requires cookie_secure = true.
# @type=enum(lax, strict, none, disabled)
GF_SECURITY_COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax

# Allow Grafana to render inside <iframe>/<embed> on other origins. Off by
# default as anti-clickjacking; only enable behind your own auth.
# @type=boolean
GF_SECURITY_ALLOW_EMBEDDING=false

# HSTS response header. Only enable when the deployment is HTTPS-only and you
# accept that browsers will refuse plain HTTP for max_age seconds.
# @type=boolean
GF_SECURITY_STRICT_TRANSPORT_SECURITY=false
# @type=number
GF_SECURITY_STRICT_TRANSPORT_SECURITY_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=86400

# Content-Security-Policy header. Off by default because custom panel plugins
# can violate the default template.
# @type=boolean
GF_SECURITY_CONTENT_SECURITY_POLICY=false

# Login brute-force protection. Leave enabled; `max_attempts` is the count
# before the account is locked out.
# @type=boolean
GF_SECURITY_DISABLE_BRUTE_FORCE_LOGIN_PROTECTION=false
# @type=number
GF_SECURITY_BRUTE_FORCE_LOGIN_PROTECTION_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5

# Stops the UI fetching avatars from gravatar.com (an outbound call keyed by a
# hash of each user's email). Turn on for air-gapped or privacy-sensitive
# installs.
# @type=boolean
GF_SECURITY_DISABLE_GRAVATAR=false

# ─── Users & sign-up ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("[users] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#users)

# Self-service registration. Off by default and should stay off on anything
# reachable from the internet.
# @type=boolean
GF_USERS_ALLOW_SIGN_UP=false

# Let non-admin users create new organizations.
# @type=boolean
GF_USERS_ALLOW_ORG_CREATE=false

# Role new users land in when auto-assigned to the default org.
# @type=enum(Viewer, Editor, Admin, None)
GF_USERS_AUTO_ASSIGN_ORG_ROLE=Viewer

# @type=boolean
GF_USERS_AUTO_ASSIGN_ORG=true

# Default UI theme and language for users who have not chosen one.
# @type=enum(dark, light, system)
GF_USERS_DEFAULT_THEME=dark
GF_USERS_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE=en-US

# ─── Authentication ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("[auth] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#auth)

# Turns off Grafana's own username/password login entirely. Only set true once
# an external provider works, or you lock yourself out.
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_DISABLE_LOGIN=false

# Hides the login FORM but keeps the login page (for OAuth-only setups where
# you still want the admin escape hatch via basic auth on the API).
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_DISABLE_LOGIN_FORM=false

# HTTP basic auth on the API. Leave enabled if anything scripted talks to
# /api with admin credentials.
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_BASIC_ENABLED=true

# Enforces a 12-character, mixed-class password policy for basic-auth users.
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_BASIC_PASSWORD_POLICY=false

# Session lifetimes. Duration strings (5m, 6h, 10d, 2w, 1M). Empty means the
# built-in defaults: 7d inactive, 30d absolute.
GF_AUTH_LOGIN_MAXIMUM_INACTIVE_LIFETIME_DURATION=
GF_AUTH_LOGIN_MAXIMUM_LIFETIME_DURATION=

# Anonymous access: anyone reaching the port gets the role below WITHOUT
# logging in. Handy for a read-only wall-mounted dashboard, dangerous
# anywhere public. `org_name` must match an existing organization exactly.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("[auth.anonymous] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#authanonymous)
GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ENABLED=false
GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ORG_NAME=Main Org.
# @type=enum(Viewer, Editor, Admin)
GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ORG_ROLE=Viewer

# Generic OAuth2 (any OIDC provider: Authentik, Keycloak, Auth0, Zitadel …).
# `enabled` plus the four endpoint/credential values are the minimum; every
# other [auth.generic_oauth] key from defaults.ini works under the same
# GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_* prefix.
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_ENABLED=false
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_NAME=OAuth
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# @sensitive
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_SCOPES=user:email
# @type=url
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_AUTH_URL=
# @type=url
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=
# @type=url
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_API_URL=
# Comma/space separated list of email domains permitted to sign in.
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=
# Create Grafana accounts for provider users who do not have one yet.
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_ALLOW_SIGN_UP=true

# Trust a reverse proxy that has already authenticated the user and passes the
# username in a header. ONLY safe when the container cannot be reached except
# through that proxy. Anyone who can hit port 3000 directly can forge the
# header and become any user.
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_PROXY_ENABLED=false
GF_AUTH_PROXY_HEADER_NAME=X-WEBAUTH-USER
# @type=enum(username, email)
GF_AUTH_PROXY_HEADER_PROPERTY=username
# @type=boolean
GF_AUTH_PROXY_AUTO_SIGN_UP=true
# Comma-separated list of IPs/CIDRs allowed to send that header.
GF_AUTH_PROXY_WHITELIST=

# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Grafana's own metadata store (dashboards, users, data sources), NOT the
# metrics store, which is Prometheus. Defaults to SQLite at
# /var/lib/grafana/grafana.db, covered by the grafana-data volume the compose
# declares; that is fine for a single instance. Point at Postgres/MySQL only
# if you run several replicas, and migrate the existing data yourself,
# because switching the type does not copy anything.
# @docs("[database] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#database)

# @type=enum(sqlite3, postgres, mysql)
GF_DATABASE_TYPE=sqlite3
# host:port of the external database, when type is not sqlite3.
GF_DATABASE_HOST=
GF_DATABASE_NAME=grafana
GF_DATABASE_USER=
# @sensitive
GF_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
# Full connection URL, an alternative to the host/name/user/password quartet.
# @sensitive
GF_DATABASE_URL=
# postgres: disable | require | verify-full. mysql: true | false | skip-verify.
GF_DATABASE_SSL_MODE=disable
# sqlite3 only: path relative to the data directory.
GF_DATABASE_PATH=grafana.db
# sqlite3 only: write-ahead logging, fewer "database is locked" errors.
# @type=boolean
GF_DATABASE_WAL=false
# @type=number
GF_DATABASE_MAX_OPEN_CONN=
# @type=number
GF_DATABASE_MAX_IDLE_CONN=2

# ─── SMTP (alerting & invites) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without this, email contact points silently do nothing and user invitations
# cannot be delivered. `host` MUST include the port.
# @docs("[smtp] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#smtp)

# @type=boolean
GF_SMTP_ENABLED=false
GF_SMTP_HOST=localhost:25
GF_SMTP_USER=
# @sensitive
GF_SMTP_PASSWORD=
# @type=email
GF_SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=admin@grafana.localhost
GF_SMTP_FROM_NAME=Grafana
# Accept a self-signed certificate from the SMTP server.
# @type=boolean
GF_SMTP_SKIP_VERIFY=false
# OpportunisticStartTLS | MandatoryStartTLS | NoStartTLS. Empty is
# opportunistic. Use MandatoryStartTLS on port 587 to refuse a downgrade.
# @type=enum(OpportunisticStartTLS, MandatoryStartTLS, NoStartTLS)
GF_SMTP_STARTTLS_POLICY=
# Hostname sent in the EHLO greeting; some relays reject the default.
GF_SMTP_EHLO_IDENTITY=

# ─── Logging ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("[log] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#log)

# The container entrypoint already forces `cfg:default.log.mode=console`, so
# logs land on stdout where `docker logs` can see them; this only overrides
# that if you also want a file log inside the container.
GF_LOG_MODE=console
# @type=enum(debug, info, warn, error)
GF_LOG_LEVEL=info
# Per-logger overrides, e.g. `sqlstore:debug,alerting.notifier:debug`.
GF_LOG_FILTERS=
# `json` makes console output machine-parseable for a log shipper.
# @type=enum(console, text, json)
GF_LOG_CONSOLE_FORMAT=console

# ─── Plugins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("Plugin management", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/administration/plugin-management/)

# Comma-separated plugin ids installed at startup, in the BACKGROUND (Grafana
# comes up first). Add `@<version>` to pin, e.g. `grafana-clock-panel@2.1.3`.
# This is the supported replacement for GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS.
GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL=

# Same list, but installed BEFORE the server starts. Use this when a
# provisioned dashboard or data source needs the plugin to exist already.
GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL_SYNC=

# Deprecated at 13.x. run.sh prints a warning and, unless
# GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS_FORCE is exactly "true", installs NOTHING. Kept declared
# because copy-pasted compose snippets still set it; migrate to
# GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL.
GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS=
# @type=boolean
GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS_FORCE=false

# Lets operators install/update plugins from inside the Grafana UI.
# @type=boolean
GF_PLUGINS_PLUGIN_ADMIN_ENABLED=true

# Comma-separated plugin ids allowed to load without a valid signature.
GF_PLUGINS_ALLOW_LOADING_UNSIGNED_PLUGINS=

# ─── Phone-home / analytics ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# @docs("[analytics] settings", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-grafana/#analytics)

# Anonymous usage counters posted to stats.grafana.org every 24h. Set false
# for an offline or privacy-sensitive install.
# @type=boolean
GF_ANALYTICS_REPORTING_ENABLED=true

# Version-check GETs to grafana.com that drive the "update available" badges.
# Both fail harmlessly with no egress, but disabling them removes the calls.
# @type=boolean
GF_ANALYTICS_CHECK_FOR_UPDATES=true
# @type=boolean
GF_ANALYTICS_CHECK_FOR_PLUGIN_UPDATES=true

# ─── Grafana container paths & secret files ───────────────────────────────
# Baked into the image; listed because the compose's grafana-data volume is
# mounted at GF_PATHS_DATA and moving these silently orphans that volume.
# @docs("Configure a Grafana Docker image", https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-docker/)

GF_PATHS_CONFIG=/etc/grafana/grafana.ini
GF_PATHS_DATA=/var/lib/grafana
GF_PATHS_HOME=/usr/share/grafana
GF_PATHS_LOGS=/var/log/grafana
GF_PATHS_PLUGINS=/var/lib/grafana/plugins
GF_PATHS_PROVISIONING=/etc/grafana/provisioning

# Docker-secret form: ANY GF_* variable also accepts a `__FILE` twin holding a
# path inside the container, and run.sh substitutes the file's contents at
# boot. Setting both a variable and its __FILE twin is a hard error that stops
# the container. Only these three are pre-declared; the pattern is general.
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD__FILE=
GF_SECURITY_SECRET_KEY__FILE=
GF_DATABASE_PASSWORD__FILE=

# Space-separated AWS profile names. For each one, run.sh reads
# GF_AWS_<profile>_ACCESS_KEY_ID / _SECRET_ACCESS_KEY / _REGION and writes
# ~/.aws/credentials for the CloudWatch data source.
GF_AWS_PROFILES=

# ─── Prometheus ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The compose sets no environment on this service, and there is nothing it
# needs. Scrape config, rules, remote write and alertmanager routing all live
# in /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml (the image ships the upstream example,
# which scrapes only Prometheus itself, so mount your own to scrape anything
# else, including Grafana's /metrics). Retention, listen address and feature
# flags are CLI flags on the container's `command:`, e.g.
# `--storage.tsdb.retention.time=30d`. The prometheus-data volume is mounted
# at /prometheus, matching the image's default --storage.tsdb.path.
# @docs("Prometheus configuration file", https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/configuration/configuration/)

# The ONLY variable the Prometheus binary reads for itself at v3.14.0 (one
# `os.Getenv` in cmd/prometheus/main.go): any non-empty value turns on Go
# block and mutex profiling at a 1-in-20 rate. Diagnostic overhead, so leave
# empty unless you are chasing a contention bug via /debug/pprof.
# @docs("Prometheus command-line flags", https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/command-line/prometheus/)
DEBUG=

# Go runtime knobs the process inherits. Prometheus prefers its own
# `--auto-gomaxprocs` / `--auto-gomemlimit` flags (both on by default at 3.x,
# derived from the container's cgroup limits), so set these only to override
# that detection. GOMEMLIMIT takes a suffixed byte value like `1GiB`.
GOMAXPROCS=
GOMEMLIMIT=

# ─── Timezone ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Container-local timezone, honoured by both images' log timestamps (the
# Grafana alpine image installs tzdata for exactly this). Grafana's UI
# renders times in each browser's own zone regardless; Prometheus stores and
# evaluates everything in UTC. This only changes what the logs read like.
TZ=UTC
